    "narrative": "For Ghost Ride the WHIP on this September 18th slate, our primary offensive engine is anchored by **Josh Naylor** (77.76) and **Brice Turang** (75.05). **Josh Naylor** enjoys the premier matchup of the day against Hughes; with a 4.55 xERA, Hughes is vulnerable, and the combination of a high-ceiling park factor and a favorable right-handed platoon setup makes **Josh Naylor** our must-start. Similarly, **Brice Turang** earns a strong projection due to an advantageous park environment and a favorable matchup against the soft-tossing Povich. While we are aggressively hunting the stolen base category, **Corbin Carroll** (62.86) remains a high-floor play despite a tough draw against Gerrit Cole. We are leaning on **Corbin Carroll**\u2019s 85% SB-success probability against Cole\u2019s slow delivery to potentially steal a crucial bag in a tight Roto race.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved **Austin Riley**, who landed just outside our active starting nine with a score of 54.26. While **Austin Riley** carries respectable power, the combination of his lower batting order position and the specific matchups faced by **Mickey Moniak** (64.31) and **Travis Bazzana** (59.62) pushed him to the pine. **Mickey Moniak** specifically benefited from a significant park-adjusted boost and a platoon advantage that, while marginal, exceeded the raw output projected for **Austin Riley**. Elsewhere, we are exercising caution with **Seiya Suzuki** (51.67) and **Gabriel Moreno** (53.14), both of whom are facing elite arms in Burns and Cole, respectively. These hitters are effectively playing for their roster spots today, as we must maintain our cap efficiency while avoiding the \"below floor\" trap that ultimately sidelined **Griffin Conine** (39.36) for today\u2019s action. Stay alert for late-breaking weather patterns, but as it stands, our lineup is optimized to prioritize stolen base upside and park-adjusted efficiency.",
